{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563328-setting-up-your-story-teller-roadmap-session-i-e-how-do-you-do-story-beats","id":1563328,"title":"Setting up your story teller roadmap (session) i.e. how do you do story beats?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-08-21T20:07:03-07:00","id":1563328,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon1.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Setting up your story teller roadmap (session) i.e. how do you do story beats?","content":"So in years past when i did DnD I wrote a LOT, like to an exhaustive degree about my settings, minor characters, options my players could take etc....<br /> <br /> Right now I feel like it lead to a lot of my burn out with being a DM so I am trying to take a different approach. I am instead trying to do most likely in order &quot;story beats&quot; which I can advance or switch to depending on how my PC's interact. I have a small smattering of them down below. They are not fleshed out almost at all but would this be a normal approach that wouldn't have me spending every minute of my day writing for this setting?<br /> <br /> <br /> Example:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Intro to newark- analysis, overview of city, description of the night life, what day or month is it what year</b></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Characters intro- Where is your character? Where do they hang out? How do we work in their involvement? Designate their relationships and the coterie and how they came to be </b></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>The Herald- The Prince\u2019s Herald arrives seemingly near similar times to each of the PC\u2019s. She announces the need of them to join together in a place of brotherhood and togetherness but one bleeding with rivalry and loud harsh tones. She refuses to elaborate. This she says is required by their Prince\u2019s hand, The scourge. She then exits wishing them their forgiveness. </b></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>How do the PC\u2019s get to their destination? (the location is chosen metatextually through group decision making) Explain why they all unanimously chose this place.</b></li> </ol><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><b>Define the location, why is it safe for vampires? Is there anyone else there? Set the mood, Is it elysium? etc\u2026</b></li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29128-internetflexing","name":"Internetflexing","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-23T02:35:51-07:00","id":1563360,"title":"","content":"There are many ways to go through this, but I think your current setup works best if understood as scenes rather than story beats. Even then some of those things could be better handled outside the story.<br /> <br /> My suggestion would be first to define things with the players. WoD differs from D&amp;D here in two important ways. First, being modern urban means it has a completely different dynamic as setting. You all know how modern countries and cities work, the characters will deal mostly with a secret conspiracy behind the scenes of civilization. Second, Backgrounds put a lot of power in the hands of the players to define their place in the city from the get go. They're not &quot;starting adventurers&quot;, and actively chose if they're kings or beggars, whom they're related to, what their individual stories will be about.<br /> <br /> With this, run the intro as prep for character creation rather than first scene. Let them bake their place within the city as part of the sheet rather than being &quot;new arrivals&quot;, unless this is a specific chronicle element.<br /> <br /> Doing introductions in the game is feasible and common, but if you're not relying on this for plot, consider working with the players for the coterie to already be formed from previous meetings. Play a prelude, a series of diceless scenes from the characters past to get some of their previous meetings in character, that'll help to have everything on board already for the actual game. If you go with them not knowing each other at first, just do the destination part as group discussion rather than a scene after the meeting with the NPC.<br /> <br /> Other than that, since the basic dynamics of a city is simpler to improvise and understand, your main &quot;setting development&quot; in a regular VtM game is a social map: note down relevant NPCs and decide how they relate, behave, and their goals, along with social position. In VtM this will be the most actually important thing to know 95% of the time.<br /> <br /> Building plots can be done through this map or as a series of scenes you can play when you feel convenient.<br /> <br /> Finally, embrace player participation. Let them decide where to go and what kind of places to seek, let them propose things the city could have, even during play invite players to be part of setting description, WoD works well with this and it will increase player engagement while it makes your work easier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-24T04:48:33-07:00","id":1563405,"title":"","content":"something that helps me flesh out chronicles is The Alexandrian node, second track &amp; scenarios methodology. Although it is thought for investigative games mainly and scenes might work best for VtM, I think it really helps to organize your chronicle while giving players freedom.<br /> <br /> Inherently many published chronicles follow it. Nodes can be scenes, PNJs, events, locations... interconected by &quot;clues&quot; that players can navigate. Independently, each PC or faction have their agenda / timeline, where you define the events that happen if the players do not get involved, and that should be revisited when players take action changing the events.<br /> <br /> While simple, it is very flexible and you can complicate as much as you want. You can google The Alexandrian page and start exploring from his gamemastering 101, but I warn you: It is a rabbit whole. Here is the link: <a href=\"https://thealexandrian.net/gamemastery-101\" target=\"_blank\">https://thealexandrian.net/gamemastery-101</a><br /> <br /> To make the setting feel dynamic with conflict, I feel that the factions segment on a Strange Adventures on creating a chronicle this was also simple and useful: <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xOsFvQhnVrw\" data-code=\"xOsFvQhnVrw\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/xOsFvQhnVrw/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div> . Basically is creating three factions in conflict and draw the relationship map.<br /> <br /> Not sure if that was what you were asking, but in order to define a roadmap without railroading your PCs (too much) it helps!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/17974-strangelove","name":"Strangelove","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar17974_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-24T09:52:36-07:00","id":1563420,"title":"","content":"I use something like the following... note this is for a session if you were asking how to structure a chronicle it's basically this but repeated over several adventures, each composed of some undefined number of sessions but usually 3-4. <br /> <br /> <b>Skeleton of a scene is:</b><br /> <br /> Scene name<br /> Description of location:<br /> NPCs at location:<br /> Lead out to other scenes:<br /> <br /> You can add more stuff if you need it (sub locations as an example) but you only need those details for a scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Skeleton of a session is:</b><br /> <br /> A namelist.<br /> 1 scenes per hour of play + 1-2 extra scenes. (so 4 scenes for 3 hours, 7-8 scenes for 6 hours).<br /> Some rando scribbles to sort your thoughts.(like context, or what's gonna happen next). <br /> <br /> And then you have some general <b>chronicle seed</b> that helps start you off, usually around 2 pages:<br /> City<br /> Vibe<br /> 3 Cool things about it<br /> 3 Major NPCs (usually 1 or 1,5 of them gets used)<br /> Each Major NPCs has: name, motivation, date of embrace, some secret, some enemy (another npc), something they want from the player.<br /> 3 Cool locations<br /> 3 Problems everyone in the city has to deal with.<br /> <br /> -<br /> <br /> A session are multiple scenes, an adventure are multiple sessions, and a chronicle is multiple adventures.<br /> Meaning if have the scene down pat you can write a chronicle, it's just repeating the same patterns.<br /> <br /> -<br /> <br /> So riffing off your example of 'new vampires, new domain' we can do an example session.<br /> Note this is just a quick example, I spend more time than I want prepping new adventures or chronicles. (weekends though, not years).<br /> <br /> <b>Example Session: New Domain - Old Blood</b><br /> <br /> Namelist:<br /> <br /> Bob Wilburt<br /> Steve Loranga<br /> Alex De Lauerentis<br /> Malcolm Gilder<br /> Hubert Mortimer<br /> Katja Lambert<br /> Anita Aillwell<br /> Petra Halbert<br /> Jennifer Rutger<br /> Evelynn Lamburg<br /> <br /> <b>Scene </b>0 -<br /> No retrospective since it's first session, everyone makes their character, present the premise to them.<br /> <i>Premise</i>: You're arriving in the city of Newark. You got a domain there but you're all unproven neonates so the domain is shit. Default is abandoned mall but if you guys have a better idea you can decide it as long as it's abandoned. A mall a factory, an old hospital an old asylum. It's either on the edge of a town or a suburb. A fixer upper, a way to prove yourself or a punishment: You decide. <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 1:</b><br /> <b>Description</b>: Whatever the location they picked the building is run down. Weeds grow on the lot. There are many buildings around but the local block is pretty abandoned. Sharp warm glows from the windows across the block. The warmth does not reach across the large lot in front of the building.<br /> <br /> They can decide how they want to arrive - good spot for them to describe their character. Can travel together or one and one.<br /> <br /> <b>NPCs</b>: There'll be some homeless squatters in the building, first issue is they'll have to decide what to do with them.<br /> Squatter 1: Old crochy guy, doesn't want to leave, will get insulting, will not get violent. High empathy character may notice he's scared of leaving. (lots of crime in the area)<br /> Squatter 2: Old crochy woman. Also doesn't want to leave, also worried, but she's not scared about going outside she knows about the corpses in the basement.<br /> <br /> <b>Lead out </b>- If they befriend the squatters one of them might tell them about how the place isn't all there and they were asked to stay here (they don't know who the guy was but they can describe them) and keep watch and hint at the basement. If the pcs drive them away they get to investigate the rest of the building, possible split up and one of them can hear a sound (from the ghost)<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2:</b><br /> <b>Description</b>: Basement is wet, there might be a small amount of flooding going on, or just condensation from a broken down ventilation system. Concrete walls. Bad lights (or no light). <br /> The basement is large and in one of the rooms there's 7 badly decayed corpses in a pile.<br /> <br /> <b>NPCs</b>: The ghost may watch them but won't make contact. If a PC has auspex maybe they'll just outright spot it (it'll disappear into a wall or something if its seen).<br /> <br /> Corpses have been here for like a year (keep time ambiguous because I don't know how quickly things decay), they still have their clothes on, if the squatters are still around they don't know much (they refuse to enter basement unless intimidated) just that they are there no one is looking for them and they know not to talk about it.<br /> <br /> The corpses are all from the same family and one of them is more decayed than the other (so a kindred and his next of kin). (really describe the grossness of rifling through their clothes. Have some insects crawl out or whatever).<br /> <br /> <b>Lead out: </b>They get their surnames from their pockets, and now they gotta decide what to do with them. If it was a larger adventure I'd give them time but for this one we're gonna have the cops show up as if by coincidence just a little while after they entered the building after never looking at the place for years. <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3: </b>They either start moving the bodies, argue about what to do with them or investigate the rest of the building.<br /> Description: Lots of decrepit walls, rusted over furniture, maybe some vermin.<br /> <br /> In the middle of whatever they're doing two cop cars pull up and enter the building. Say they got a tip of bodies, were going to ask the squatters questions, want to know who the hell the pcs are. If anyone actually tell them they just bought the building (they either own it or one of their sires do, decide based on what charactes they make) the cops will be more confused about what's going on. <br /> <br /> Pcs can handle them however they want but they're not going to get convinced so if they want them to just leave they need to use disciplines. Also while two questions the pcs, two more will question the squatters if they're still around (maybe even if driven off they lingered outside), or else start searching the place (they got a warrant). <br /> <br /> The pcs can handle it however they want if the scene lags or they actually for some reason decide to show them the bodies the ghost will start chucking debris at the cops. So then the pcs will have to handle panicking cops and a masquerade breach.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4:</b><br /> <br /> Probably just wing it from here.<br /> <br /> If something needs to happen have the ghost possess and steal one of the corpses and the pcs notice its missing and that's another thing they need to figure out. And in general all attempts at removing the corpses fail for mysterious and clearly supernatural reasons. <br /> <br /> Context:<br /> The corpses were the family of a kindred who lived in the city, Hubert Mortimer. He and everyone he loved was brutally murdered by another kindred due to a plot.<br /> Huberts friends called the cops on the pcs, believing they were allied with his murderer. In reality they just got the domain because the previous holder had vanished. Prince had no clue Hubert was in the basement, the people he sent to check the place got dominated by the murderer. The murderer didn't know the domain was handed to someone else so he's panicking. He tried to remove the corpses a couple of times but the ghost always screwed it up for him. He placed the squatters there to just have an eye on things. He was working on some way to get rid of it.<br /> The ghost is the grandmother of the family who wants to take revenge for the deaths of all her loved once. Decide fetters latter (maybe the corpses, probably the corpses). She can be really powerful in the building and nearly helpless outside it or something. <br /> <br /> -<br /> <br /> Took like an hour. Probably could spend two more on it, flesh it out a bit. Bit more descriptions and such. But good enough for an example. <br /> -<br /> <br /> Oh stats for npcs.<br /> <br /> * Squatters 2-4 dice, difficult 6 to hit them or intimidate them or such. <br /> * Ghost: 8 dice on ghost stuff, 4-6 on stuff she can do from her mortal days. Diff 7 to interact with. Not materialized so can't actually hit it or anything. Is able to levitate objects and posses corpses because that's creepy and fits horror. <br /> * Police: 4-6 dice on police stuff. Maybe one of them has a personal interest. 6 difficulty to interact with.<br /> Health pool is 1 or 4 or N/A for the ghost. Ghost can be called Petra Hubert.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}